WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122
Keys help us answer a basic question: which row are we talking about?

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:08.507
A primary key is the official row identifier selected for a table.

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:15.032
A natural key comes from the business domain, such as a medical record number or national provider identifier.

00:00:15.032 --> 00:00:19.242
A surrogate key is generated by the database and has no business meaning.

00:00:19.242 --> 00:00:25.162
An alternate key is another candidate identifier that still needs uniqueness even though it is not the primary

00:00:25.162 --> 00:00:28.212
key. A composite key uses several columns together.

00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:31.057
A foreign key references a key in another table.

00:00:31.057 --> 00:00:33.617
Do not think of all keys as interchangeable.

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:37.332
They solve different problems: identity, business uniqueness, and relationships.

00:00:37.332 --> 00:00:42.372
As we continue, pay attention to which key is being used for each purpose.

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:45.202
Before drawing tables, write one sentence that defines what one row represents.

00:00:45.202 --> 00:00:48.252
That grain statement prevents many later problems.

00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:53.267
If one row represents a patient, repeated encounters do not belong as extra columns in the same row;

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:57.920
they are a different entity with a one-to-many relationship.
